counterexample:
No executable INSERT statements can be provided that create different results without violating the NOT NULL constraint on table1.c7. As an illustration, if constraints were ignored, the following INSERTs would create a counterexample:
INSERT INTO table4 (c15, c8, c7) VALUES (1, '2012-08-25', NULL);
INSERT INTO table1 (c7, c6) VALUES (NULL, 'EUR');
With this data, the INNER JOIN would include the row where T1.c7 is NULL (since T1.c7 = T2.c7 with both NULL), making COUNT(T1.c7) = 0 (as NULL is not counted) and COUNT(*) = 1, resulting in different outputs. But this violates the NOT NULL constraint on table1.c7.

